// The language server, spoken to over JSON-RPC exactly as an editor would.
//
//     burxt build editors/lsp/drive-lsp.bx -o star-drive-lsp
//     STAR_CHECK=./star-check ./star-drive-lsp
//
// **A server tested only by opening an editor is a server whose protocol handling is a guess.** This
// speaks the messages that matter, on a document with a real problem in each of the three layers, and
// asserts the diagnostics come back on the right lines.
//
// ---- and it was JavaScript, in `drive-lsp.mjs` --------------------------------------------------
//
// It moved for the reason the server beside it moved — Andre's directive, *"I will not accept a
// language that really depends on another language"* — and it had to move IN THE SAME CHANGE. A driver
// is what says the server works; leaving the driver in Node would have meant the file proving `node`
// is unnecessary could only be run by `node`, which is the same joke `tools/languages.bx` refuses
// about a Python program auditing Python.
//
// ---- one frame at a time became one stream, and that is a real design change -------------------
//
// The JavaScript held the server open and awaited each reply before sending the next request. **Burxt
// cannot do that, and the reason is specific: there is no `popen` and no `pipe`.** `os_capture_status`
// runs a command to completion; `os_run` gives an exit code. Nothing in the standard library hands
// back a pair of file descriptors to a live child, so a Burxt program cannot hold a conversation with
// one.
//
// It does not need to. **Not one request here depends on a reply**, because the protocol under test is
// request/response with no server-initiated negotiation: the `initialize` reply is read, never
// branched on. So the whole session is written to a file, the server is run once with its stdin on it,
// and the reply frames are parsed out of its stdout afterwards. Every message is still framed, every
// `Content-Length` is still honoured, and the replies are still matched to the requests that caused
// them by position.
//
// **What batching gains, stated because it is not obvious.** Every frame arrives in ONE read, which is
// a case the awaiting driver never produced and an editor certainly can — and it is the case the
// JavaScript SERVER cannot survive: it publishes diagnostics from a `.then()`, so `process.exit(0)` on
// the `exit` notification fires before any of them flush. Fed this same file, `star-lsp.mjs` answers
// `initialize` and `shutdown` and drops all six diagnostic frames on the floor. The Burxt server is
// synchronous throughout and answers every one.
//
// **What batching loses, and how it is bought back.** A batch cannot see whether the server answered
// before the stream ended — a server that read to EOF first and then replied would pass every
// assertion below. That is not a small distinction: it is the whole difference between a server and a
// filter, and `lib/os.bx` says it beside `os_read_line` — reading to EOF is not a slow version of
// answering, it is a deadlock. So one assertion runs a second session through a pipe that **withholds
// the second frame until the first has been answered**, and cannot pass unless the server replies, and
// flushes, mid-stream. `timeout` is what stops that from hanging if it does not.
use "std/files.bx";
use "std/string.bx";
use "std/os.bx";
use "std/json.bx";

// How many verdicts a run reaches. **A number CHECKED against at the bottom, not a comment** — and it
// was a comment for the length of one test run, which is how this paragraph got written: it said 12
// while the run printed 13, and nothing noticed because the number was only ever read on the way out
// of a failure. A count nobody compares is a decoration.
//
// It is worth comparing because of how this driver fails. It reads its verdicts out of parsed frames,
// and the failure mode of a frame parser that breaks is not a wrong answer, it is NO answer — every
// lookup returns the empty String and a whole scenario can go quiet. `refs.bx`'s rule: a suite that
// found nothing is not a suite that passed.
const ASSERTIONS: Int = 14;

// The reply frames one session produces: `initialize`, one `publishDiagnostics` per document opened,
// and `shutdown`. **`refs.bx`'s rule — a suite that found nothing is not a suite that passed** — so
// this is asserted rather than assumed, and it is the first assertion for that reason.
const FRAMES: Int = 6;

pure function contains(haystack: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    return string_find(haystack, needle) >= 0;
}

// ---- the documents under test -------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A `const` may name a literal and these are literals, but they are functions anyway: a top-level
// `let` is invisible to every function body, and the documents are needed inside `run`.
//
// Every `{` and `}` is escaped, which is the whole shape of writing a `.sbmx` document inside a Burxt
// string — a slot is `\{\{ … \}\}`.

// 1. A well-formed component with a typo in a slot — the layer only the compiler can find, reached
//    through the GENERATED component and mapped back by name.
pure function document_typo() -> String allocates {
    return ":props: count: Int\n:!props:\n\nAt \{\{ to_string(cuont) \}\}.\n";
}

// 2. Not a component: an unknown block. star's own rule, already positioned.
pure function document_unknown() -> String allocates {
    return ":props: n: Int\n:!props:\n\n:mystery:\nhi\n:!mystery:\n";
}

// 3. Not a document: a missing fence. BMX's rule.
pure function document_fence() -> String allocates {
    return ":props: n: Int\n:!props:\n\n:div:\nno closing fence\n";
}

// 4. A clean component, which must report nothing — see `run` for why it is asserted last.
pure function document_clean() -> String allocates {
    return ":props: count: Int\n:!props:\n\nAt \{\{ to_string(count) \}\}.\n";
}

// ---- framing ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure function frame(message: Json) -> String allocates {
    let body: String = json_render(message);
    return "Content-Length: " + to_string(len(body)) + "\r\n\r\n" + body;
}

pure function request(id: Int, method: String) -> String allocates {
    let mutable fields: [Field] = [];
    let a: Int = push(fields, json_field("jsonrpc", json_text("2.0")));
    let b: Int = push(fields, json_field("id", json_int(id)));
    let c: Int = push(fields, json_field("method", json_text(method)));
    return frame(json_object(fields));
}

pure function notification(method: String) -> String allocates {
    let mutable fields: [Field] = [];
    let a: Int = push(fields, json_field("jsonrpc", json_text("2.0")));
    let b: Int = push(fields, json_field("method", json_text(method)));
    return frame(json_object(fields));
}

// `textDocument/didOpen`, built through `json.bx` rather than by concatenation so the document's
// quotes, backslashes and newlines are escaped by the same code that will read them back.
pure function did_open(uri: String, text: String) -> String allocates {
    let mutable document: [Field] = [];
    let d1: Int = push(document, json_field("uri", json_text(uri)));
    let d2: Int = push(document, json_field("languageId", json_text("sbmx")));
    let d3: Int = push(document, json_field("version", json_int(1)));
    let d4: Int = push(document, json_field("text", json_text(text)));
    let mutable params: [Field] = [];
    let p1: Int = push(params, json_field("textDocument", json_object(document)));
    let mutable fields: [Field] = [];
    let a: Int = push(fields, json_field("jsonrpc", json_text("2.0")));
    let b: Int = push(fields, json_field("method", json_text("textDocument/didOpen")));
    let c: Int = push(fields, json_field("params", json_object(params)));
    return frame(json_object(fields));
}

// Every reply frame in a stream of them, in order. A malformed stream answers what it managed to read
// rather than a refusal — the frame COUNT is asserted, so a short read is reported as the count it is
// and not as six scenarios that quietly said nothing.
//
// ---- and the header is CHECKED against what follows it, not trusted -------------------------------
//
// This used to read `Content-Length`, slice that many bytes, and move on. **A server declaring the
// wrong length would have been believed**, which is not hypothetical: the language session found
// `lib/cgi.bx` declaring `len(body) + 1` on every response it had ever sent — deliberately and
// correctly, because `print` appended a newline that really was on the wire. When `print_exact` removed
// the newline, the `+ 1` became a lie, and the same wall in `star-lsp.bx` was closed in the same
// release. **The framing here changed in that commit**, from a `putchar` loop to `print_exact`, and
// nothing in this driver would have noticed a byte either way.
//
// Their fix is the technique worth copying, and it is not "update the expected bytes". A fixture that
// pins the literal response catches a change and cannot say what is wrong with it; asserting the
// RELATION — the declared count against the bytes that actually follow — survives the document changing
// and fails with the diagnosis. Their two controls gave opposite errors, `says 13 and 12 bytes follow`
// against `says 12 and 13 bytes follow`, where a byte-fixture would have said only that something moved.
//
// Here the relation is checkable without a second parse: after consuming exactly `length` bytes, what
// remains must be empty or must begin the next header. One byte over and the next frame starts mid-word;
// one byte under and a stray `\r` or `}` sits in front of it.
function frames_in(stream: String, mutable out: [Json], mutable faults: [String]) -> Int allocates {
    let mutable framing: String = "";
    let mutable at: Int = 0;
    let mutable reading: Bool = true;
    while reading {
        let rest: String = substring(stream, at, len(stream) - at);
        let split: Int = string_find(rest, "\r\n\r\n");
        if split < 0 {
            reading = false;
        } else {
            let header: String = string_to_lower_ascii(substring(rest, 0, split));
            let mark: Int = string_find(header, "content-length:");
            if mark < 0 {
                reading = false;
            } else {
                let after: String = substring(header, mark + 15, len(header) - mark - 15);
                let ends: Int = string_find(after, "\r\n");
                let mutable said: String = after;
                if ends >= 0 { said = substring(after, 0, ends); }
                let length: Int = string_to_int(string_trim(said), 0);
                if split + 4 + length > len(rest) {
                    reading = false;
                } else {
                    let body: String = substring(rest, split + 4, length);
                    match json_parse(body) {
                        Error(why) => { reading = false; }
                        Ok(message) => { let a: Int = push(out, message); }
                    }
                    at = at + split + 4 + length;

                    // The relation, checked at every boundary. `framing_error` is read by the caller,
                    // which is why this does not simply stop: a driver that goes quiet on a bad frame
                    // reports the same thing as a server that sent none.
                    let remaining: String = substring(stream, at, len(stream) - at);
                    if len(remaining) > 0 && !string_starts_with(remaining, "Content-Length:") {
                        let mutable shown: Int = 24;
                        if len(remaining) < shown { shown = len(remaining); }
                        framing = framing + "frame " + to_string(len(out)) + " declared "
                            + to_string(length) + " bytes, and what follows is not the next header"
                            + " but `" + substring(remaining, 0, shown) + "`; ";
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    if len(framing) > 0 {
        let f: Int = push(faults, framing);
    }
    return len(out);
}

// ---- reading a reply ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A missing field answers the empty String rather than a refusal, and every assertion below is a
// String comparison for the same reason the JavaScript's was (`String(got) === String(want)`): a
// diagnostic that is absent and one that is on the wrong line should both FAIL, and both should print
// what arrived.

pure function child(value: Json, name: String) -> Json {
    match json_at(value, name) {
        None => { return json_null(); }
        Some(found) => { return found; }
    }
}

pure function text_at(value: Json, name: String) -> String {
    match json_as_text(child(value, name)) {
        None => { return ""; }
        Some(said) => { return said; }
    }
}

pure function number_at(value: Json, name: String) -> String {
    match json_as_int(child(value, name)) {
        None => { return ""; }
        Some(found) => { return to_string(found); }
    }
}

function list_in(value: Json, mutable out: [Json]) -> Int allocates {
    match value {
        List(items) => {
            let mutable i: Int = 0;
            while i < len(items) {
                let a: Int = push(out, items[i]);
                i += 1;
            }
        }
        Truth(ignored) => { }
        Number(ignored) => { }
        Text(ignored) => { }
        Object(ignored) => { }
        Null => { }
    }
    return len(out);
}

// The diagnostics of one `publishDiagnostics` frame.
function diagnostics_of(message: Json, mutable out: [Json]) -> Int allocates {
    return list_in(child(child(message, "params"), "diagnostics"), out);
}

// The zero-based line a diagnostic starts on, as a String, or `""` when there is no diagnostic — so
// an absent one fails the line assertion instead of throwing before it.
pure function start_line(diagnostics: [Json]) -> String {
    if len(diagnostics) == 0 { return ""; }
    return number_at(child(child(diagnostics[0], "range"), "start"), "line");
}

pure function first_code(diagnostics: [Json]) -> String {
    if len(diagnostics) == 0 { return ""; }
    return text_at(diagnostics[0], "code");
}

pure function first_message(diagnostics: [Json]) -> String {
    if len(diagnostics) == 0 { return ""; }
    return text_at(diagnostics[0], "message");
}

// ---- the verdicts -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **Printed as it is decided and recorded at the same time.** Burxt has no closures, so the tally
// cannot hide in the reporter — the array is threaded, which is what the framework does with state
// and for the same reason.
//
// **Every verdict is recorded, not just the failing ones**, so the length of the array answers "how
// many questions were asked" and not only "how many went wrong". Those are different numbers and it is
// the first one that catches a scenario which stopped running.
function verdict(what: String, got: String, want: String, mutable made: [String]) -> Bool {
    if got == want {
        print("  ok   " + what);
        let a: Int = push(made, "ok " + what);
        return true;
    }
    let b: Int = push(made, "FAIL " + what);
    print("  FAIL " + what);
    print("       got  " + got);
    print("       want " + want);
    return false;
}

pure function failed(made: [String]) -> Int {
    let mutable count: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(made) {
        if string_starts_with(made[i], "FAIL ") { count += 1; }
        i += 1;
    }
    return count;
}

pure function truth(value: Bool) -> String {
    if value { return "true"; }
    return "false";
}

// ---- running the server -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The whole session, as the bytes an editor would send.
pure function session(uri: String) -> String allocates {
    return request(1, "initialize")
        + did_open(uri, document_typo())
        + did_open(uri, document_unknown())
        + did_open(uri, document_fence())
        + did_open(uri, document_clean())
        + request(2, "shutdown")
        + notification("exit");
}

// **The one assertion a batch cannot make, made with a pipe that holds its tongue.**
//
// The subshell writes the first frame, then waits for the reply FILE to become non-empty before
// writing the second. A server that read its input to EOF before answering would never let that wait
// finish, so the only way two frames come back is if the first was answered — and flushed — while the
// stream was still open. `timeout` is what turns that deadlock into a failed assertion instead of a
// hung suite.
//
// Written as a script file and run with `sh`, rather than quoted into one command line: a JSON frame
// is full of double quotes and a nested `sh -c` string would need two layers of escaping for no gain.
function answers_mid_stream(work: String, server: String, checker: String) -> Bool
        touches commands, files, input {
    let first: String = work + "/first.frames";
    let second: String = work + "/second.frames";
    let replies: String = work + "/mid.replies";
    let script: String = work + "/mid.sh";
    let w1: Int = file_write(first, request(1, "initialize"));
    let w2: Int = file_write(second, request(2, "shutdown") + notification("exit"));
    let w3: Int = file_write(script,
        "( cat " + file_quote(first) + "\n"
        + "  while [ ! -s " + file_quote(replies) + " ]; do sleep 0.05; done\n"
        + "  cat " + file_quote(second) + "\n"
        + ") | STAR_CHECK=" + file_quote(checker) + " " + file_quote(server)
        + " > " + file_quote(replies) + " 2>/dev/null\n");
    let ran: Capture = os_capture_status("timeout 10 sh " + file_quote(script));
    let mutable answered: [Json] = [];
    let mutable ignored_faults: [String] = [];
    let counted: Int = frames_in(file_read(replies), answered, ignored_faults);
    return counted == 2;
}

let checker: String = os_env_or("STAR_CHECK", "star-check");
// The built binary, beside every other tool this repository builds. Overridable because
// `tests/extension.py` asks the same question of a server installed somewhere else.
let server: String = os_env_or("STAR_LSP", "./star-lsp");

let mutable made: [String] = [];

match file_temp_directory("drive-lsp") {
    None => {
        print("no scratch directory, so the session cannot be written");
        exit(2);
    }
    Some(work) => {
        // A document beside the real examples, so component imports resolve the way they would in a
        // project. It is never created: the server writes its own scratch copy beside it, which is the
        // mechanism under test.
        let mutable here: String = ".";
        match os_cwd() {
            None => { here = "."; }
            Some(found) => { here = found; }
        }
        let uri: String = "file://" + here + "/examples/.lsp-probe.sbmx";

        let requests: String = work + "/session.frames";
        let wrote: Int = file_write(requests, session(uri));
        let ran: Capture = os_capture_status(
            "STAR_CHECK=" + file_quote(checker) + " " + file_quote(server)
            + " < " + file_quote(requests));

        let mutable replies: [Json] = [];
        let mutable faults: [String] = [];
        let counted: Int = frames_in(ran.output, replies, faults);

        // **The declared length against the bytes that follow it**, before any verdict is read out of
        // the frames — because a frame that parsed is not a frame that was framed correctly, and the
        // whole point of the relation is that it fails with the diagnosis rather than with a diff.
        let framed: Bool = verdict("every frame's Content-Length is the bytes that follow it",
                            to_string(len(faults)), "0", made);
        if len(faults) > 0 {
            print("       " + string_join(faults, ""));
        }

        // First, because every verdict below is read out of this list and a short one would make them
        // all say nothing rather than something wrong.
        let ok: Bool = verdict("the server answers every frame in the session",
                          to_string(counted), to_string(FRAMES), made);
        if counted != FRAMES {
            print("");
            print("the session was answered " + to_string(counted) + " frame(s) deep, so the "
                + "assertions below have nothing to read. What it said on stderr:");
            print(ran.errors);
            exit(1);
        }

        let ready: Json = child(replies[0], "result");
        let a1: Bool = verdict("the server answers initialize",
                          text_at(child(ready, "serverInfo"), "name"), "star-lsp", made);

        // **This asserts the NAME above, which is the half that never changes.** The server reported
        // version `0.1.0` while the released tag was `v0.2.0`, and a client LOGS this field — so every
        // bug report about a diagnostic would have named the wrong version, and nothing here would have
        // said so. The markup session hit the identical defect in theirs and told me to grep for it.
        //
        // Read out of `burxt.package` rather than written down, because a driver that hardcoded the
        // version would agree with a server that hardcoded a different one.
        let manifest: String = file_read("burxt.package");
        let mutable wanted: String = "";
        let rows: [String] = string_split(manifest, "\n");
        let mutable r: Int = 0;
        while r < len(rows) {
            if string_starts_with(rows[r], "version") && len(wanted) == 0 {
                let fields: [String] = string_split_space(rows[r]);
                if len(fields) > 1 { wanted = fields[1]; }
            }
            r += 1;
        }
        let a2: Bool = verdict("and reports the version the package declares, rather than one of its own",
                          text_at(child(ready, "serverInfo"), "version"), wanted, made);
        let a3: Bool = verdict("it offers text sync",
                          number_at(child(ready, "capabilities"), "textDocumentSync"), "1", made);

        // 1. A well-formed component with a typo in a slot — the layer only the compiler can find.
        let mutable typo: [Json] = [];
        let t: Int = diagnostics_of(replies[1], typo);
        let a4: Bool = verdict("a slot typo is reported", truth(len(typo) >= 1), "true", made);
        let a5: Bool = verdict("  …on the line the author wrote it", start_line(typo), "3", made);
        let a6: Bool = verdict("  …naming what is wrong",
                          truth(contains(first_message(typo), "cuont")), "true", made);

        // 2. Not a component: an unknown block. star's own rule, already positioned.
        let mutable unknown: [Json] = [];
        let u: Int = diagnostics_of(replies[2], unknown);
        let a7: Bool = verdict("an unknown block is reported with its code",
                          first_code(unknown), "STAR-E001", made);
        let a8: Bool = verdict("  …on its fence", start_line(unknown), "3", made);

        // 3. Not a document: a missing fence. BMX's rule.
        let mutable fence: [Json] = [];
        let f: Int = diagnostics_of(replies[3], fence);
        let a9: Bool = verdict("a malformed document is reported with BMX's code",
                          truth(string_starts_with(first_code(fence), "BMX-E")), "true", made);

        // 4. **BMX's structural WARNINGS are gone, and the loss is asserted rather than left silent.**
        //    The JavaScript server imported `lint` from `reference/bmx.js` and merged BMX-W001 in at
        //    severity 2; BMX's Burxt implementation has the parser and no lint, so a Burxt server can
        //    only get them by spawning `node` — which is the one dependency this whole port removes.
        //    The two assertions that stood here (a warning is reported; it is on the right line below a
        //    `===bx` section) are blocked on `bmx_lint` existing in `bmx/burxt/bmx.bx`.
        //
        //    **A capability that quietly stopped appearing is the failure this replaces.** So the
        //    server must SAY the warnings are absent, and it says it on stderr, which
        //    `os_capture_status` keeps apart from the protocol on stdout — a log line written to stdout
        //    would be read as a frame.
        let a10: Bool = verdict("and says out loud that BMX's warnings are absent, rather than dropping them"
                           + " silently",
                           truth(contains(ran.errors, "BMX's structural warnings are absent")),
                           "true", made);

        // 5. That it answered at all while the stream was open — see `answers_mid_stream`.
        let a11: Bool = verdict("it answers a frame before the input ends, which is what makes it a server",
                           truth(answers_mid_stream(work, server, checker)), "true", made);

        // 6. A clean document reports nothing. **Asserted LAST, after every case that proves the
        //    server can find something**, because a server that is broken end to end — a checker that
        //    never runs, a parser that reads no output, a `publish` that sends an empty list — produces
        //    exactly this answer too. It is the one verdict that is worthless on its own.
        let mutable clean: [Json] = [];
        let c: Int = diagnostics_of(replies[4], clean);
        let a12: Bool = verdict("a clean component reports nothing", to_string(len(clean)), "0", made);

        print("");
        // The count before the verdicts, because a run that asked the wrong number of questions has
        // not earned the right to report on the answers.
        if len(made) != ASSERTIONS {
            print("this driver reached " + to_string(len(made)) + " verdict(s) where it declares "
                + to_string(ASSERTIONS) + " — a scenario has stopped asserting, which reads exactly"
                + " like success");
            exit(1);
        }
        if failed(made) > 0 {
            print(to_string(failed(made)) + " failure(s) of " + to_string(ASSERTIONS));
            exit(1);
        }
        print("the server speaks the protocol, in " + to_string(ASSERTIONS) + " assertions");
    }
}
